statement:
A contiguous part of an array `a` (a subarray `a[l..r]`) is called a **mountain** if all of the following hold:

- it has at least `3` elements;
- there is a peak index `p` with `l < p < r`;
- the values strictly increase up to the peak: `a[l] < a[l+1] < ... < a[p]`;
- the values strictly decrease after the peak: `a[p] > a[p+1] > ... > a[r]`.

Given the array, print the length of the **longest mountain** subarray. If the array contains no mountain, print `0`.

Equal neighbouring values break a slope: a plateau can never be part of a mountain.

**Follow-up:** Can you solve it with a single pass over the array and `O(1)` extra space?

